{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Unstable as water, you shall not excel.</b> Pachaz (פַּחַז) is a noun, from \"empty and reckless men\" (וּפוֹחֲזִים) (Judges 9:4) — empty and impulsive, light-headed. And our Sages use it frequently: \"I looked in my mirror and my instinct pachaz (פָּחַז) upon me\" (Nedarim 9b), meaning my instinct leapt upon me; and they further said (Sanhedrin 57a), \"it is through their pachizuta (פְּחִיזָא, recklessness) that they reveal,\" meaning the lightness of their mind; \"a pachiza (פְּחִיזָא) people, whose mouth precedes their ear\" — \"you still persist in your pachizuta (פַּחְזוּתַיְכוּ)\" (Shabbat 88a) — impulsive, light-headed.\n\nIt is also possible that it is the reverse of haste (חִפָּזוֹן). He says: a flood that comes like water — do not excel in it, for you went up onto your father's bed in recklessness and lightness of mind; then you profaned it — \"my bed he went up\" (יְצוּעִי עָלָה) — your pachaz (פַּחַז), which is like water that rises and floods, went up. This is like the language Scripture uses for water: \"Behold, HaShem brings up upon them the mighty and abundant waters of the River, and it shall rise over all its channels and go over all its banks, and it shall sweep through Judah and flood and pass over\" (Isaiah 8:7–8).\n\nOur Sages said (cf. Shabbat 55a), \"my bed he went up\" — the Shekhinah, whose way it is to go up on my bed (יְצוּעִי). And Scripture says (1 Chronicles 5:1), \"when he profaned his father's bed\" (וּבְחַלְּלוֹ יְצוּעֵי אָבִיו), so that the bed (יְצוּעַ) is the thing profaned — or perhaps he calls it by that name. And if taken as a euphemism, it is possible that \"the one who went up\" (הָעוֹלֶה) is Jacob himself, in the language of \"if I go up upon the bed of my resting\" (אִם אֶעֱלֶה עַל עֶרֶשׂ יְצוּעָי) (Psalms 132:3), and he says: \"then you profaned me,\" but he spoke in hidden language, out of honor. And likewise \"when he profaned his father's bed\" (וּבְחַלְּלוֹ יְצוּעֵי אָבִיו) as a euphemism: when he who goes up profaned his father's bed.\n\nFrom this verse, according to its plain sense, it appears as I explained in the portion of Vayishlah (Ramban on Genesis 35:22): that Reuben intended to disqualify Bilhah from his father so that she would not bear him more sons and thereby diminish his birthright. Therefore he said to him that this is recklessness and lightness of mind — to think to gain profit from it, yet no advantage would come to him from it, only loss."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The word פחז and its forms are consistently left in Hebrew with parenthetical translation, following the instruction to preserve Hebrew forms the author quotes; I rendered the Aramaic phrases from the Talmudic citations into English but kept their original Hebrew/Aramaic script in parentheses."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:00.781860501Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}